===Noun===
{{en-noun|skull and crossbones|skulls and crossbones}}

# A [[depiction]] of a [[human#Adjective|human]] [[skull]] and [[two]] [[crossed#Adjective|crossed]] [[femur]]s ([[thighbone]]s), a [[symbol#Noun|symbol]] of [[death]] [[traditionally]] [[use#Verb|used]] on the [[Jolly Roger]] pirate flag, but now as a [[warning#Noun|warning]] of [[toxicity]] or other [[danger#Noun|dangers]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Tale of Galway, Ireland|editor={{w|Louis Antoine Godey}}|magazine=[[w:Godey's Lady's Book|The Lady’s Book: A Monthly Magazine of Belles-lettres and the Arts.{{nb...|Containing a Choice and Multifarious Variety of Original and Selected Literary Compositions. Embellished with Several Hundred Different Engravings. Designed and Executed by the Most Eminent Artists; with Beautiful Illustrations of the Prevailing Fashions, splendidly Coloured. And Various Patterns for Ornamental Embroidery. In addition to which Its Contents Embrace a Number of the Most Popular Melodies, Set to Music, and Arranged for the Piano Forte.}}]]|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=L[ouis] A[ntoine] Godey & Co.{{nb...|Franklin Place.}}|month=February|year=1832|page=95|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EdURAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA95|column=1|oclc=1041857864|passage=In an obscure corner of the town stands a house of extreme antiquity, over the door of which are still to be seen a '''skull and crossbones'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Zanoni|volume=III|chapter=VII|page=87|passage=She stood within the chamber, and gazed around her; no signs by which an Inquisitor of old could have detected the Scholar of the Black Arts were visible. No crucibles and caldrons, no brass-bound volumes and ciphered girdles, no '''skulls and crossbones'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Mardi|volume=II|chapter=They Visit the Lords Piko and Hello|page=144|passage=Escorted to the sign of the '''Skull-and-Cross-Bones''', we received the very best entertainment which that royal inn could afford.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Letter to the Editors|editors=E. W. Evans [''et al.'']|magazine=[[w:Yale Literary Magazine|The Yale Literary Magazine:{{nb...|Conducted by the Students of Yale College.}}]]|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher=T. J. Stafford [for] A. H. Maltby|month=June|year=1850|volume=XV|issue=VII|page=273|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=g68gAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA273|oclc=504152729|passage=Mark those bottles, my dear sirs, as housewives do corrosive sublimate or ratsbane,—with a '''skull and crossbones''', and label them "Poison," for the effect of them upon the luckless wight who should taste their contents, would be convulsive, very.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Isaac Elton|authorlink=Charles Elton (lawyer)|author2=Mary Augusta Elton|chapter=France: Early Bookmen—Royal Collectors|title={{w|The Great Book-Collectors}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Kegan Paul|Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner & Co. Ltd.]]|year=1893|page=107|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pJUDAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA107|oclc=|passage=His own taste combined the gloomy and the grotesque, his clothes and his [book] bindings alike being covered with '''skulls and crossbones''', and spangles to represent tears, with other conventional emblems of sorrow.}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Bill the Conqueror|chapter=Sunday Night at Mario's|section=1|page=254|passage=And this was open rebellion. This was hoisting the '''skull and cross-bones'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Brunner|authorlink=|chapter=IX|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Umiszdry47MC&pg=PT31|title=[[w:Double, Double (Brunner novel)|Double, Double]]|location=London|publisher=Gateway, [[w:Hachette (publisher)|Hachette]]|year=1969|year_published=2011|isbn=978-0-575-10149-4|passage=Overhead, the black flag with the white '''skull-and-crossbones''' symbolizing defiance of radio regulations fluttered limply atop the two-hundred-foot mast mounted on the converted coaster from which they operated.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard C. Colton|author2=Edmund S. Ward|author3=Charles H. Wager|title=Practical Handbook of Industrial Traffic Management|edition=5th|location=Washington|publisher=Traffic Service Corporation|year=1973|page=579|oclc=714024|passage=When poisons are involved, red '''skull and crossbones''' are added.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William G. Boltz|title=The Origin and Early Development of the Chinese Writing System|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher={{w|American Oriental Society}}|year=1994|page=16|isbn=978-0-940490-78-9|passage=The '''skull and crossbones''' is, to be sure, a visual sign that communicates a very specific meaning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=The Regiment de Tren: Transportation under Fire|editors={{w|Cary Nelson}} and Jefferson Hendricks|title=Madrid 1937: Letters of the Abraham Lincoln Brigade from the Spanish Civil War|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=1996|year_published=2003|page=554|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3yHKAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA554|isbn=978-0-415-91408-6|passage=On the floor was a big flag whose significance I could not make out. There was a big red cross in the center and around it were two '''skull and crossbones''' and two other insignia with crossed torches, crossed scythes, and a smaller cross in the center.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Flynn|authorlink=Michael Flynn (writer)|chapter=Friends in High Places|editor=[[w:David G. Hartwell|David G[eddes] Hartwell]]|title=Rogue Star|series=[[w:Tor Books|A Tor Book]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Tom Doherty|Tom Doherty Associates]]|month=April|year=1998|page=144|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/roguestar00flyn/page/144/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-312-86136-0|passage=Because when I saw him again, the Bird had two '''skull-and-crossbones''' stenciled on his X-ray camera.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael J. Kalsher|author2=Michael S. Wogalter|chapter=Warnings: Hazard Control Methods for Caregivers and Children|editors=Rani Lueder and Valerie J. Berg Rice|title=Ergonomics for Children: Designing Products and Places for Toddlers to Teens|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher={{w|Taylor & Francis}}|year=2007|section=section D (Children and Product Design)|page=531|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YzMtzvs9sBMC&pg=PA531|isbn=978-0-415-30474-0|passage=[C]hildren who are not told otherwise may interpret the ubiquitous '''skull and crossbones''' pictograph {{...}} to mean "pirate food" {{...}} Parents can also play an important role by teaching their children that the '''skull and crossbones''' symbol is sometimes used to indicate poison.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Alice Rawsthorn|authorlink=Alice Rawsthorn|title=Skull and crossbones as branding tool|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220510000449/https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/02/arts/02iht-design02.html|archivedate=10 May 2022|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|The New York Times Company}}|date=1 May 2011|issn=0362-4331|oclc=971436363|passage=By the turn of the 18th century, when Captain [John] Cranby espied [[w:Emanuel Wynn|[Emanuel] Wynn]]'s '''skull and crossbones''', the piracy trade was flourishing and ambitious pirates were becoming increasingly sophisticated in the way they operated.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William E[dwin] Thompson|chapter=Biker and Motorcyclist Values, Old and New|title=Hogs, Blogs, Leathers and Lattes: The Sociology of Modern American Motorcycling|location=Jefferson, N.C.|publisher={{w|McFarland & Company}}|year=2012|page=60|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_hash_i-a9MC&pg=PA60|isbn=978-0-7864-6859-1|passage=These [motorcycle] helmets often resemble World War I Prussian helmets, or are modified half-helmets, usually plain black in color, or adorned with '''skulls and crossbones''', or other intimidating symbols.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jen Jones [''i.e.'', Jennifer Lynn Jones]|chapter=1|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WNmeAwAAQBAJ&pg=PT13|title=The New Ashley|series=Sleepover Girls|location=North Mankato, Minn.|publisher=[[w:Capstone Publishers|Capstone Young Readers]]|year=2015|isbn=978-1-62370-252-6|passage=It was a quilted leather tote with tiny '''skulls-and-crossbones''' inside each diamond; Sirena's style was definitely impossible to miss. But the million-dollar question was: how had this girl snagged it? Everyone online was buzzing about this purse, but no one could get one.}}

